\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Unit quaternions and binary rotation groups}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{View the Hamilton quaternions as a right vector space over
#{\mathbb C=\operatorname{span}_{\mathbb R}\{1,i\}}. Left
multiplication by a quaternion is complex-linear for that right
structure. \ref{fdip-00M6} chooses the ordered basis in which this
action of a unit quaternion is special unitary, with the matrix
recorded there. The unit quaternions are therefore isomorphic to
#{SU(2)}. Conjugation on the imaginary quaternions defines a
surjective homomorphism
##{\pi:SU(2)\longrightarrow SO(3),\qquad
 \pi(q)(v)=qvq^{-1},\qquad\ker\pi=\{1,-1\}.}
For the proper rotation groups #{R_T,R_O,R_I} of the regular
tetrahedron, octahedron and icosahedron, define
##{2T=\pi^{-1}(R_T),\qquad
 2O=\pi^{-1}(R_O),\qquad 2I=\pi^{-1}(R_I).}
Their orders are respectively #{24,48,120}. Each quotient by
#{\{1,-1\}} is its indicated rotation group.}

\proof{
\p{Let #{M} be the matrix map of \ref{fdip-00M6}. The images of #{i,j,k} satisfy the quaternion multiplication
relations, so #{M} is an injective real-algebra homomorphism.
Direct calculation gives
##{M(q)^*M(q)=|q|^2I,\qquad\det M(q)=|q|^2.}
Conversely, a special unitary matrix of degree two has the form
##{\begin{pmatrix}z&w\\-\overline w&\overline z\end{pmatrix},
 \qquad |z|^2+|w|^2=1,}
by orthonormality of its columns and determinant one. Taking real and
imaginary parts of #{z,w} gives a unique unit quaternion. This proves
the group identification.}

\p{Conjugation preserves imaginary quaternions and their norm,
so gives an orthogonal map of #{\mathbb R^3}. Its determinant is
one: it depends continuously on the unit quaternion, the sphere
#{S^3} is connected, and at #{q=1} it equals one. A quaternion
commuting with all of #{i,j,k} is real, so the kernel on unit
quaternions is #{\{1,-1\}}. More explicitly, for a unit imaginary
quaternion #{n} and #{q=\cos(\theta /2)+n\sin(\theta /2)},
quaternion multiplication gives
##{qvq^{-1}=v\cos\theta+(n\times v)\sin\theta
 +n(n\cdot v)(1-\cos\theta).}
This is rotation through #{\theta} about #{n}. Every element of
#{SO(3)} has an axis: its real orthogonal eigenvalues include one,
and its restriction to the perpendicular plane is a planar rotation.
The displayed formula therefore proves surjectivity.}

\p{The proper rotations of a regular solid act transitively on its
vertices. A rotation fixing a vertex must rotate its incident edges
cyclically about the center-to-vertex axis; all those cyclic rotations
are symmetries. The tetrahedron has 4 vertices with stabilizer
order three; the octahedron has 6 with stabilizer order 4; the
icosahedron has 12 with stabilizer order 5. Orbit–stabilizer
therefore gives #{|R_T|=12}, #{|R_O|=24}, #{|R_I|=60}.
Every rotation has exactly two lifts under #{\pi}, giving the stated
binary group orders and quotients.}
}

\texfig{\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=large,row sep=large]
\pi^{-1}(R) \arrow[r,hook] \arrow[d,"2:1"'] &
 SU(2) \arrow[d,"\pi"] \\
R \arrow[r,hook] & SO(3)
\end{tikzcd}}

\p{For any of the three rotation groups #{R}, the square records
the subgroup and its central double cover. In particular, the
[Hurwitz-unit model](fgap-000K) of #{2T} is this tetrahedral
preimage. Reduction of the Hurwitz order modulo #{3} identifies this
group with #{\operatorname{SL}(2,\mathbb{F}_3)}, by \ref{fdip-00MK}.
\ref{fdip-00M6} checks that its generators preserve a
regular tetrahedron, so its 24 elements exhaust the preimage. This
identifies the group used in the
[finite spinor gauge example](fdip-006Y). The matrix equivalence
does not assert the #{SO(3)} quotient or the three binary subgroup
identifications; those have their separate arguments above. A finite
subgroup supplies an available group action, not a physical
interpretation of that action.}

\transclude{fdip-00M6}
